WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.380
How do we prove NP-completeness?

2
00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:04.620
We will separate membership from

3
00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:07.860
reduction-based hardness, use SAT as

4
00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:10.080
a concrete decision example, and

5
00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:11.460
derive the consequence of a

6
00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:13.920
polynomial solver for any NP-complete

7
00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:14.400
problem.

8
00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:16.760
A decision problem D is

9
00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:18.520
NP-complete when it belongs to

10
00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:20.540
NP and every problem in

11
00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:22.740
NP has a polynomial-time reduction

12
00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:24.740
to D. The first condition

13
00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:27.760
provides efficiently checked yes-certificates, while

14
00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:29.040
the second makes D at

15
00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:30.220
least as hard as all

16
00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:32.020
NP problems under these reductions.

17
00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:33.960
To prove a new target

18
00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:36.720
B NP-complete, normally start by

19
00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:39.320
describing a polynomial-length certificate and

20
00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:41.960
a polynomial verifier. Then reduce

21
00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:44.440
an already known NP-complete problem

22
00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:46.580
A to B, preserving yes

23
00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:48.520
exactly in both directions.

24
00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:50.900
Why does one known source

25
00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:52.680
suffice for the second condition?

26
00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:55.800
Every NP problem already reduces

27
00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:58.260
to A, and transitivity composes

28
00:00:58.260 --> 00:00:59.680
that reduction with the new

29
00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:01.400
transformation from A to B.

30
00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:04.080
For SAT, the input is

31
00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:05.680
a Boolean formula and the

32
00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:07.560
question is whether some assignment

33
00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:09.600
makes it true. A proposed

34
00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:11.500
assignment is a short certificate

35
00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:12.800
that can be checked by

36
00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:14.740
evaluating the formula's operations.

37
00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:17.800
Consider A or B, and

38
00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:19.940
not A or C, with

39
00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:21.280
the two clauses joined by

40
00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:22.760
and. Set A true, B

41
00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:24.620
false and C true: the

42
00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:25.900
first clause is true from

43
00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:27.500
A, the second is true

44
00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:29.320
from C, and their conjunction

45
00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:30.160
is true.

46
00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:33.060
One satisfying assignment proves that

47
00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:34.840
this formula is a yes-instance,

48
00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:36.580
without needing to find the

49
00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:38.840
best assignment or test every

50
00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:41.400
possibility. An assignment failing one

51
00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:43.460
clause only rejects that candidate,

52
00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:45.140
just as in our earlier

53
00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:46.260
certificate examples.

54
00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:49.080
The course takes circuit satisfiability

55
00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:51.120
as a known NP-complete problem

56
00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:53.100
and discusses its reduction to

57
00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.700
SAT. Together with SAT membership

58
00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:57.580
in NP, that supplies the

59
00:01:57.580 --> 00:01:59.440
two obligations needed to classify

60
00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:01.180
SAT as NP-complete.

61
00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:03.420
The full construction from an

62
00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:05.720
arbitrary polynomial verifier to a

63
00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:08.120
circuit is explicitly outside this

64
00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:09.700
course scope. We use the

65
00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:11.200
stated theorem here rather than

66
00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:12.740
pretending that a small hand-drawn

67
00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:14.340
circuit proves the theorem for

68
00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:15.300
all NP problems.

69
00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:17.580
A reduction from a known

70
00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:20.300
NP-complete source without a target-membership

71
00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:22.680
proof establishes the hardness part

72
00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:25.320
only. Conversely, a verifier alone

73
00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:27.720
establishes membership but says nothing

74
00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:28.780
about being as hard as

75
00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:29.960
every other NP problem.

76
00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:33.160
All NP-complete decision problems reduce

77
00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:34.660
to one another through polynomial

78
00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:37.540
transformations. This equivalence does not

79
00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:39.160
mean their best implementations have

80
00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:42.260
identical constants, polynomial exponents, or

81
00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:44.200
practical behaviour on typical instances.

82
00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:46.900
If even one NP-complete problem

83
00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:49.620
has a deterministic polynomial-time algorithm,

84
00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:52.200
every NP problem inherits one

85
00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:54.040
through its reduction. Since P

86
00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:55.600
is already contained in NP,

87
00:02:56.020 --> 00:02:57.800
that would establish P equals

88
00:02:57.800 --> 00:02:59.780
NP, whose truth is currently

89
00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:01.260
unresolved in this framework.

90
00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:03.400
Things to take note of:

91
00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:06.060
classify the decision version, show

92
00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:08.700
membership, choose a known NP-complete

93
00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:10.720
source and point the reduction

94
00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:12.980
toward your target. State the

95
00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:15.480
transformation and both correctness directions,

96
00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:17.980
then use transitivity; a prestigious

97
00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:19.400
problem name is not a

98
00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:20.840
substitute for either half of

99
00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:22.260
the proof, hmph.
